<h2>3. Threats (STRIDE)</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>STRIDE</th><th>Threat / abuse case</th><th>Component</th></tr>
<tr><td>T1</td><td>Spoofing</td><td>Credential stuffing and brute force on the login form using breached passwords</td><td>API /login</td></tr>
<tr><td>T2</td><td>Spoofing</td><td>Session cookie theft via XSS or sniffing over HTTP</td><td>Browser, proxy</td></tr>
<tr><td>T3</td><td>Tampering</td><td>SQL injection through note search or ID parameters</td><td>API, DB</td></tr>
<tr><td>T4</td><td>Tampering</td><td>Malicious dependency or compromised npm package alters app behavior</td><td>Supply chain</td></tr>
<tr><td>T5</td><td>Repudiation</td><td>Admin or user actions (delete, export) not logged, so incidents cannot be traced</td><td>API</td></tr>
<tr><td>T6</td><td>Info disclosure</td><td>IDOR: user changes <code>/notes/123</code> to <code>/notes/124</code> and reads another user's note</td><td>API</td></tr>
<tr><td>T7</td><td>Info disclosure</td><td>Secrets committed to Git or exposed in verbose error messages / debug mode</td><td>Secrets, API</td></tr>
<tr><td>T8</td><td>Info disclosure</td><td>Uploaded files stored in a public bucket or served with guessable URLs</td><td>File storage</td></tr>
<tr><td>T9</td><td>Denial of service</td><td>Request flooding, huge uploads, or expensive queries exhaust the server</td><td>Proxy, API</td></tr>
<tr><td>T10</td><td>Elevation of privilege</td><td>Normal user calls admin endpoints because role checks are missing server-side</td><td>API</td></tr>
<tr><td>T11</td><td>Elevation of privilege</td><td>Malicious file upload (script/HTML) executed or rendered in the app's origin</td><td>File storage</td></tr>
<tr><td>T12</td><td>Spoofing</td><td>Password-reset token is guessable, long-lived, or reusable (account takeover)</td><td>Email flow</td></tr>
</table></div>

