<h2>5. Prioritized hardening plan with verification</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th>Priority</th><th>Control</th><th>How to verify</th></tr>
<tr><td>P1 (T6, T10)</td><td>Server-side authorization on every request: check <code>note.owner_id == session.user_id</code>; enforce roles on admin routes.</td><td>Log in as user A, request user B's note ID: expect 403/404. Call an admin route as a normal user: expect 403.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1 (T1)</td><td>Rate-limit login, lockout/backoff, hash passwords with Argon2id or bcrypt, offer MFA.</td><td>Send 20 wrong logins: expect throttling. Check DB: no plaintext or fast-hash passwords.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1 (T3)</td><td>Parameterized queries / ORM only; validate input types.</td><td>Code search for string-concatenated SQL; run a SAST tool (e.g. Semgrep) and confirm zero findings.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P1 (T12)</td><td>Reset tokens: random 128-bit+, single-use, expire in 15 min, stored hashed.</td><td>Use a token twice and after expiry: expect rejection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2 (T2)</td><td>HTTPS + HSTS; cookies <code>Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax</code>; CSP; output encoding.</td><td>Inspect cookie flags in DevTools; check headers with securityheaders.com or curl -I.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2 (T7)</td><td>Secrets in a secret manager or env vars, never in Git; debug off in production; generic error pages.</td><td>Run gitleaks on repo history; trigger an error and confirm no stack trace shown.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2 (T8, T11)</td><td>Private bucket, signed short-lived URLs, allow-list file types, size limit, serve from separate domain with <code>Content-Disposition: attachment</code>.</td><td>Open a file URL while logged out: expect denied. Upload a .html file: confirm it is rejected or downloaded, not rendered.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P2 (T4)</td><td>Lockfile, <code>npm audit</code> / Dependabot, minimal dependencies.</td><td>CI fails on high-severity advisories.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3 (T9)</td><td>Proxy rate limits, upload size caps, request timeouts.</td><td>Burst test your own staging server and confirm 429 responses.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P3 (T5)</td><td>Structured audit logs for login, reset, delete, export, admin actions; alert on anomalies.</td><td>Perform each action in staging and confirm a log entry with user ID and timestamp.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2>6. Short explanation</h2>
<div class="card">I modeled NoteBox by identifying what attackers would want (credentials, private notes, secrets), drawing where data crosses trust boundaries, and applying STRIDE to each component. The highest risks are broken access control, credential attacks, injection, and weak reset flows because they are common in real attacks and expose user data directly. The plan fixes those first, then hardens configuration, then adds monitoring, and every control has a concrete check so progress can be verified.</div>
